Description: Serve the challenge to clients that do not accept gzip
 RenderIndex refuses one challenged request in 64, at random, when the
 client does not list gzip in Accept-Encoding: it answers with the page
 "client_error_browser" at the policy's DENY status (HTTP 500 before
 1.28.0). A client without Accept-Encoding accepts any coding (RFC 9110,
 section 12.5.3), and real clients send none: curl by default, some
 accessibility and archiving tools, proxies that strip the header. They
 get an error page at random while the proof of work, the actual defence,
 is never reached. Keel Web saw it on its own test client
 (Keel-Linux/keel-web#2). Keel's policy gives DENY the status 200, so on
 1.28 such a client gets an error page with HTTP 200.
 .
 Upstream history: the sampled refusal came with #501 ("ensure that
 clients store cookies", https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis/pull/501);
 #1958 (https://github.com/TecharoHQ/anubis/pull/1958, in 1.28.0)
 changed its answer from 500 to the policy's DENY status and logged it at
 INFO, and left the sampling itself open: its commit says "whether the
 rejection should be sampled needs its own discussion". The sampling is
 still in 1.28.2. The heuristic stops no scraper that sends
 "Accept-Encoding: gzip", which costs nothing; every client without gzip
 still has to solve the proof of work.
 .
 This patch drops the sampled refusal, so such a client gets the
 challenge every time, and adds a test that 4096 requests without gzip,
 with identity and with br, all get the challenge page (status and body:
 the body, because a policy may give the refusal the same status). It
 removes upstream's TestNonGzipClientGetsDenyStatus (#1958), which
 asserts the opposite: that at least one of 4096 such requests is
 refused, and that the refusal is logged at INFO. With the refusal gone
 there is nothing for that test to check; the new test is its inverse.
 .
 Keel-only for now; upstreamable as a question, not as a pull request:
 upstream keeps the check on purpose. It can be dropped when upstream
 removes the sampling or makes it optional, or when Keel Web adds
 "Accept-Encoding: gzip" in front of Anubis and a real test shows that
 curl gets the challenge every time.
